Per-install modes for ops/Caddyfile, copied into the proxy image at
/etc/caddy/modes/.

TLS mode, picked by TLS_MODE in .env and imported as <TLS_MODE>.global and
<TLS_MODE>.site:

  none  default. A domain in SITE_ADDRESS gets an ordinary Let's Encrypt
        certificate automatically; ":5550" serves plain http (LAN, or behind
        a tunnel / edge proxy that terminates TLS).
  ip    the server is reached by a bare public IP (WAN IP + port forwarding).
        Let's Encrypt issues IP certificates only on its 6-day "shortlived"
        profile (Caddy renews every ~2 days). Behind NAT the connection
        arrives on a LAN/container address and browsers send no SNI for an
        IP, so default_sni tells Caddy which certificate to present.
        Needs Caddy 2.11.4+ and inbound TCP 80 or 443 for validation.

Edge proxy, picked by EDGE_PROXY in .env and imported as
edge-<EDGE_PROXY>.servers inside the global `servers` block. It decides whose
word Caddy takes for the client's IP address (the X-Real-IP the API keys rate
limits, bans, audit entries and sessions on):

  none        default. Caddy is the edge (every ./install.sh mode). Nobody is
              trusted; the client address is the TCP peer and request headers
              cannot change it.
  cloudflare  the instance is reached only through Cloudflare (proxied DNS or
              a Cloudflare Tunnel, like zekktris.com). Trusts Cloudflare's
              edge ranges plus the Docker bridge range (where a host-run
              cloudflared connects from) and reads Cf-Connecting-Ip. Do not
              set it unless ALL outside traffic comes through Cloudflare:
              a direct visitor arriving from a trusted range could then forge
              its address.
